    return [

    /* ───────────────────────── HOME LOAN CALCULATOR ───────────────────────── */
    'home-loan-calculator' => [
        'focus'     => 'home loan calculator',
        'secondary' => [ 'how to calculate a home loan payment', 'home loan calculator with taxes and insurance', 'home loan EMI calculator', 'how much house can I afford', 'home loan calculator with PMI' ],
        'title'     => 'Home Loan Calculator: Monthly Payment, Taxes & PMI',
        'meta'      => 'Free home loan calculator: enter your loan amount, down payment, rate and term to see your full monthly payment, PMI, taxes, insurance and amortization.',
        'excerpt'   => 'Work out your full home loan payment, including property tax, insurance and PMI, then see the amortization schedule and total interest.',
        'answer'    => 'To calculate a home loan payment, use M = P[r(1+r)^n] / [(1+r)^n - 1], where P is the loan amount, r is the monthly interest rate, and n is the number of monthly payments. On a $320,000 loan (a $400,000 home with 20% down) at 7% for 30 years, principal and interest is about $2,129 a month. Add property tax, homeowners insurance, and PMI if your down payment is under 20% for your total payment.',
        'steps'     => [
            'Enter the home price and your down payment, in dollars or as a percentage.',
            'Add the interest rate your lender quoted and the loan term in years.',
            'Enter yearly property tax and homeowners insurance, plus any HOA dues, so the payment reflects the real monthly cost.',
            'If your down payment is under 20%, leave the PMI rate on; the calculator removes it automatically once your balance reaches 80% of the home price.',
            'Read your monthly payment breakdown, the amortization summary and the total interest you will pay over the life of the loan.',
        ],
        'formula'   => '<p>A home loan payment is calculated with the standard amortization formula:</p><p><strong>M = P × [r(1+r)^n] ÷ [(1+r)^n − 1]</strong></p><p>where <strong>M</strong> is the monthly principal-and-interest payment, <strong>P</strong> is the loan amount (home price minus down payment), <strong>r</strong> is the monthly interest rate (annual rate ÷ 12), and <strong>n</strong> is the total number of monthly payments (loan term in years × 12). Property tax, homeowners insurance, PMI and HOA dues are added on top of M to give the full monthly housing payment, often called PITI (principal, interest, taxes, insurance).</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>Home price $400,000, 20% down ($80,000), loan amount $320,000, rate 7%, 30-year term:</p><ul><li>Monthly rate: 7% ÷ 12 = <strong>0.5833%</strong></li><li>Number of payments: 30 × 12 = <strong>360</strong></li><li>Principal and interest: <strong>$2,129/month</strong></li><li>Property tax ($4,800/yr) + insurance ($1,800/yr): <strong>$550/month</strong></li><li>No PMI (20% down): <strong>$0</strong></li><li>Total monthly payment: <strong>$2,679</strong></li><li>Total interest paid over 30 years: <strong>$446,435</strong></li></ul><p>With only 10% down ($40,000) at the same rate, PMI at 0.6% a year adds about $180 a month until the balance falls to 80% of the home price. Keep the same $320,000 loan but switch to a 15-year term at a slightly lower 6.5% rate often available for shorter terms, and the principal-and-interest payment rises to roughly $2,789 a month — about $660 more — but total interest drops to around $182,000, a saving of over $264,000 compared with the 30-year schedule. That trade-off between a lower payment now and lower total cost over time is the single biggest decision a home loan term involves.</p>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'How to calculate a home loan payment by hand', '<p>You can estimate the payment yourself with the formula above, but most people let a calculator handle the exponents. The two numbers that move the payment the most are the loan amount (price minus down payment) and the interest rate — a 1-point rate difference on a $320,000 loan changes the payment by roughly $200 a month. The loan term changes total interest more than it changes the payment: a 15-year loan at the same rate carries a higher monthly payment but can cut total interest by more than half.</p>' ],
            [ 'Home loan calculator with taxes and insurance', '<p>A payment quote that only shows principal and interest understates what you will actually owe each month. Lenders typically collect one-twelfth of your annual property tax and homeowners insurance with every payment and hold it in an escrow account, then pay the bills when due. This calculator adds both, plus PMI and HOA dues where they apply, so the number you see matches what shows up on a loan estimate.</p>' ],
            [ 'PMI: when it applies and when it drops off', '<p>Private mortgage insurance protects the lender, not you, and usually applies when your down payment is under 20% of the home price. It is charged as a percentage of the loan balance per year, split into monthly payments. By federal law, PMI must be canceled once your loan balance reaches 78% of the original home value if you are current on payments, and you can usually request its removal at 80%. This calculator removes PMI automatically once the balance crosses that line.</p>' ],
            [ 'How the loan term changes total interest', '<p>Amortization means a fixed payment that is split differently between principal and interest each month: early payments are mostly interest, later payments are mostly principal. A 30-year term keeps the monthly payment lower, which helps affordability, but the longer timeline means more total interest. A 15-year term shifts dramatically more of each early payment toward principal, often three to four times as much as the same point in a 30-year loan, and can save tens of thousands of dollars in interest, provided the higher payment still fits your budget.</p>' ],
            [ 'How much house can I afford', '<p>Most lenders want your total housing payment (PITI) at or below about 28% of your gross monthly income, and all debts including housing at or below 36% to 43%. Run a few home prices through this calculator against your income and other debts before you start shopping, so you know your realistic range before a lender pulls your credit.</p>' ],
            [ 'Fixed-rate vs adjustable-rate: why the rate you enter matters', '<p>This calculator assumes a fixed rate that does not change for the life of the loan, which is how most home loans in the U.S. are structured and the easiest to budget around. An adjustable-rate loan starts with a lower introductory rate for a set period, then resets periodically based on a market index, so the payment can rise (or fall) later. If a lender quotes you an adjustable rate, run the calculator once with the introductory rate and again with a higher rate you could reasonably see after a reset, so you are not budgeting only around the best-case number.</p>' ],
            [ 'This is an estimate, not a loan offer', '<p>This tool is an educational calculator, not a lender, a loan originator or a source of personalized financial advice. It does not check your credit, verify your income, or guarantee any rate, payment or approval. Actual loan terms depend on your lender\'s underwriting, your credit profile, local property tax rates and insurance costs, and current market pricing, all of which this calculator cannot see. Use the numbers here to compare scenarios and prepare questions for a licensed loan officer, not as a substitute for a real loan estimate.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [
            [ 'Principal', 'The amount you borrow, not counting interest.' ],
            [ 'PITI', 'Principal, interest, taxes and insurance — the full monthly housing payment.' ],
            [ 'PMI', 'Private mortgage insurance, usually required with less than 20% down.' ],
            [ 'Amortization', 'The schedule that splits each payment between interest and principal over the loan term.' ],
            [ 'Escrow', 'An account your lender uses to collect and pay your property tax and insurance.' ],
        ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'How do I calculate a home loan payment myself?', 'Use M = P[r(1+r)^n] ÷ [(1+r)^n − 1], where P is the loan amount, r is the monthly rate (annual rate ÷ 12) and n is the number of monthly payments. On a $320,000 loan at 7% for 30 years, that works out to about $2,129 a month before taxes and insurance.' ],
            [ 'What is included in a home loan calculator with taxes and insurance?', 'A full calculator adds property tax, homeowners insurance, PMI (if your down payment is under 20%) and any HOA dues to the base principal-and-interest payment, so the total matches what a lender would actually collect each month.' ],
            [ 'How much down payment do I need for a home loan?', 'There is no fixed minimum across all loans — conventional loans allow as little as 3% to 5% down, FHA loans allow 3.5%, and VA loans allow 0% for eligible borrowers. Putting down less than 20% on a conventional loan usually triggers PMI until your equity reaches 20%.' ],
            [ 'Is a 15-year or 30-year home loan better?', 'A 30-year loan has a lower monthly payment and more flexibility; a 15-year loan has a higher payment but usually a lower rate and far less total interest. Compare both terms in the calculator with your real numbers before deciding.' ],
            [ 'When does PMI come off a home loan?', 'By law, PMI must be canceled once your loan balance reaches 78% of the original home value if your payments are current, and you can request removal once you reach 80% equity, often by requesting a new appraisal.' ],
            [ 'What is the difference between a home loan calculator and a mortgage calculator?', 'They calculate the same math — both use the standard amortization formula for a home purchase loan. "Home loan" and "mortgage" are different names for the same product in everyday use.' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [
            [ 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau: Owning a home', 'https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/' ],
            [ 'CFPB: What is private mortgage insurance?', 'https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-private-mortgage-insurance-en-123/' ],
            [ 'CFPB: Homeowners Protection Act (PMI cancellation rules)', 'https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/when-can-i-remove-private-mortgage-insurance-pmi-from-my-loan-en-1944/' ],
        ],
        'related'   => [ 'mortgage-calculator', 'refinance-calculator', 'loan-affordability-calculator', 'fha-loan-calculator' ],
    ],

    /* ───────────────────────── TAX & BONUS CALCULATOR ───────────────────────── */
    'tax-bonus-calculator' => [
        'focus'     => 'tax and bonus calculator',
        'secondary' => [ 'how is a bonus taxed', 'bonus tax rate 2026', 'bonus tax calculator percentage method', 'aggregate method bonus tax', 'how much tax is taken out of a bonus' ],
        'title'     => 'Tax & Bonus Calculator (Percentage vs Aggregate Method)',
        'meta'      => 'Free tax and bonus calculator: see federal, state, Social Security and Medicare withholding on your bonus with both the percentage and aggregate methods.',
        'excerpt'   => 'Find out how much tax comes out of your bonus using the IRS percentage method or the aggregate method, including FICA and state withholding.',
        'answer'    => 'The IRS treats a bonus as a supplemental wage and, under the percentage method, withholds a flat 22% federal rate on bonuses up to $1 million in a year and 37% on any amount above that, plus 6.2% Social Security and 1.45% Medicare. On a $10,000 bonus with no state tax, percentage-method withholding is about $2,965 (22% federal plus 6.2% Social Security and 1.45% Medicare), leaving roughly $7,035 take-home. The aggregate method, which combines the bonus with a regular paycheck, can withhold more or less depending on your tax bracket.',
        'steps'     => [
            'Enter your bonus amount and, if you want to see the aggregate method, your regular pay for that period.',
            'Choose your filing status, pay frequency and the state withholding option that matches where you work.',
            'Enter your year-to-date income and Social Security wages so the calculator can check the $1 million supplemental-wage threshold and the Social Security wage base.',
            'Pick the IRS percentage method or the aggregate method to see which one your employer is likely using.',
            'Read your net take-home bonus, the federal, state and FICA withholding, and what the other method would have withheld instead.',
        ],
        'formula'   => '<p>How is a bonus taxed? The IRS calls a bonus a <strong>supplemental wage</strong> and allows employers to withhold it one of two ways:</p><p><strong>Percentage method:</strong> federal withholding = bonus × 22% for the portion of your supplemental wages up to $1,000,000 for the year, and × 37% for any amount above $1,000,000 — a flat rate that ignores your regular tax bracket.</p><p><strong>Aggregate method:</strong> the bonus is added to one regular paycheck, withholding is computed on the combined total using the IRS wage-bracket tables for your filing status and pay frequency, then the tax already withheld on the regular paycheck alone is subtracted. The remainder is the tax withheld from the bonus.</p><p>Both methods also withhold <strong>FICA tax</strong>: 6.2% Social Security (only up to the annual Social Security wage base) and 1.45% Medicare, plus an extra 0.9% Medicare surtax once an employer withholds it on wages it pays you above $200,000 in the year — employers use that flat $200,000 trigger for everyone, regardless of filing status, since they don\'t know your household\'s full tax picture. (On your actual tax return, the surtax threshold is $250,000 for joint filers and $125,000 for married filing separately, which can mean a true-up either way when you file.) State withholding, where it applies, is usually its own flat supplemental rate.</p>',
        'example'   => '<p>Say a single filer earning a steady salary gets a $10,000 year-end bonus, has no state income tax to worry about, and has already earned $60,000 this year — comfortably under both the Social Security wage base and the $1 million supplemental-wage threshold. Under the percentage method, the employer withholds federal tax at a flat 22%, which comes to $2,200. Social Security takes another 6.2% ($620) and Medicare 1.45% ($145). Nothing is owed to the state in this example, so total withholding lands at $2,965 and the bonus arrives as $7,035 in net pay. Move the same bonus to a state with a 10.23% flat supplemental rate — California, for instance — and roughly $1,023 more disappears before the deposit hits, dropping net pay to about $6,012. Whether the aggregate method would have taken more or less than that flat 22% federal slice depends entirely on which tax bracket the recipient\'s combined income actually falls into.</p>',
        'sections'  => [
            [ 'Social Security and Medicare still apply to a bonus', '<p>A bonus is not exempt from payroll tax just because it is irregular income. FICA tax applies exactly as it does to a regular paycheck: 6.2% Social Security and 1.45% Medicare, matched by the employer. The one twist is the Social Security wage base — once year-to-date wages cross that annual cap, Social Security tax stops being withheld for the rest of the year, bonus included. High earners also face an extra 0.9% Medicare surtax, which the employer withholds once your wages from that employer cross $200,000 in the year (the same $200,000 trigger applies no matter your filing status, since that\'s the employer-withholding rule, not the final tax-return threshold) and which the employer does not match.</p>' ],
            [ 'State rules are not uniform', '<p>States that tax wages generally publish their own flat supplemental-wage rate for bonuses rather than running them through the regular wage-bracket tables — New York\'s rate sits around 11.70%, California\'s around 10.23%. States with no wage income tax at all, among them Texas, Florida and Washington, withhold nothing from a bonus beyond the federal and FICA amounts. Because these figures move, check the current rate with the state\'s own revenue department before relying on it.</p>' ],
            [ 'Percentage method or aggregate — why employers pick one', '<p>The percentage method is the default for most payroll systems because it is a single flat calculation: 22% federal on supplemental wages up to $1,000,000 for the year, 37% above that line, with no reference to the employee\'s actual bracket. The aggregate method shows up more often when a bonus rides along in the same check as regular pay; it folds the bonus into that paycheck, runs the combined total through the IRS wage-bracket tables, and backs out what the regular wages alone would have owed. For someone whose marginal bracket sits above 22%, aggregate withholding can come out noticeably higher than the flat rate — and lower for someone below it.</p>' ],
            [ 'What withholding does and does not settle', '<p>Neither method decides the final tax bill. Payroll withholding is just a deposit against taxes that get reconciled on the annual return, where the bonus is ordinary income taxed at the recipient\'s real marginal rate alongside salary. Over-withholding at 22% comes back as part of a refund; under-withholding — common once someone is solidly in the 32% bracket or higher — shows up as a balance due. A larger 401(k) or HSA contribution in that same pay period, or well-timed deductible expenses later in the year, are the two most direct ways to soften that bill.</p>' ],
            [ 'Both methods, side by side, for a higher earner', '<p>Take a biweekly paycheck of $4,000 (about $104,000 a year) with a $10,000 bonus added on top, single filer. Under the <strong>percentage method</strong>, federal withholding on the bonus alone is a flat 22%, or $2,200, regardless of the $4,000 regular paycheck. Under the <strong>aggregate method</strong>, the employer instead combines the $4,000 and $10,000 into one $14,000 "paycheck," calculates federal withholding on that combined amount using the IRS wage-bracket tables for a biweekly single filer, and then subtracts what would have been withheld on the $4,000 alone. Because $14,000 biweekly pushes well into higher withholding brackets, the aggregate method typically withholds noticeably more than $2,200 on the bonus portion in this scenario — often $400 to $900 more, depending on the exact bracket breakpoints in effect. Neither number is "wrong"; they are two IRS-approved ways to withhold, and both get reconciled against the same tax bill when the return is filed.</p>' ],
            [ 'This is a withholding estimate, not tax advice', '<p>This calculator is an educational tool that estimates payroll withholding using published IRS and FICA rates; it is not tax preparation, tax advice, or a substitute for a licensed CPA or enrolled agent. It cannot see your full tax return — deductions, credits, other income or filing changes can all move your actual liability away from what was withheld from a single bonus. State supplemental rates shown are commonly published flat rates, not a lookup of your specific employer\'s payroll setup, and can change; confirm the current figure with your state\'s revenue department before relying on it for a financial decision.</p>' ],
        ],
        'terms'     => [
            [ 'Supplemental wage', 'IRS term for pay outside regular wages, including bonuses, commissions and severance.' ],
            [ 'Percentage method', 'A flat 22%/37% federal withholding rate applied to supplemental wages.' ],
            [ 'Aggregate method', 'Withholding computed by combining the bonus with a regular paycheck and taxing the total at marginal rates.' ],
            [ 'FICA', 'Social Security (6.2%) and Medicare (1.45%) payroll tax.' ],
            [ 'Social Security wage base', 'The yearly cap on wages subject to the 6.2% Social Security tax.' ],
        ],
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'How is a bonus taxed?', 'A bonus is taxed as a supplemental wage. Employers withhold it using either the percentage method (a flat 22% federal rate, 37% above $1 million for the year) or the aggregate method (combined with a regular paycheck and taxed at your marginal rate), plus Social Security, Medicare and any state withholding.' ],
            [ 'What is the bonus tax rate for 2026?', 'The federal percentage-method rate is a flat 22% on supplemental wages up to $1,000,000 in the year, and 37% on the amount above $1,000,000. FICA adds 6.2% Social Security (up to the wage base) and 1.45% Medicare on top.' ],
            [ 'Why was my bonus taxed more than my regular pay?', 'It is usually withheld at a higher flat rate (22% or more) than your paycheck\'s average withholding, or taxed at your marginal rate under the aggregate method. It is not taxed twice — any over-withholding is reconciled when you file your tax return.' ],
            [ 'Does my bonus affect my regular tax bracket?', 'A bonus adds to your total income for the year and is taxed at your marginal rate on your tax return, which can push some of the bonus into a higher bracket if it is large enough, even though payroll withholding uses a flat rate up front.' ],
            [ 'Which method withholds less tax, percentage or aggregate?', 'It depends on your income. The flat 22% percentage method withholds less than the aggregate method if your marginal tax bracket is above 22%, and more if your bracket is below 22%. Use the calculator\'s comparison figure to see which applies to you.' ],
            [ 'Is a bonus subject to Social Security and Medicare tax?', 'Yes. Bonuses are subject to the same 6.2% Social Security tax (up to the annual wage base) and 1.45% Medicare tax as regular wages, plus a 0.9% Medicare surtax that employers withhold once your wages from them cross $200,000 in the year (the threshold on your actual tax return differs by filing status, at $250,000 for joint filers and $125,000 for married filing separately).' ],
        ],
        'sources'   => [
            [ 'IRS Publication 15 (Circular E), Employer\'s Tax Guide — supplemental wages', 'https://www.irs.gov/publications/p15' ],
            [ 'IRS: Topic No. 414, Supplemental wages', 'https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc414' ],
            [ 'Social Security Administration: Contribution and benefit base', 'https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/cbb.html' ],
        ],
        'related'   => [ 'income-tax-calculator', 'self-employment-tax-calculator', 'irs-penalty-calculator', 'capital-gains-tax-calculator' ],
    ],

    ];
}
